# End-users of deployed systems (didn't choose the tool)

When a tool is deployed TO people rather than chosen BY them, initial use runs on defaults, salient recommendations, and norms; sustained use still requires fit, habit, and low friction. Defaults buy trial; only value keeps it.

Do:
- Ship good defaults and a low-friction first path; make the sanctioned way the easy way.
- Pair descriptive with injunctive norms: "most teams here do X, and it's the recommended practice." A bare descriptive norm normalizes whatever it describes.
- Build clear mental models with honest limitations — these users didn't opt in, so trust erodes fast after visible errors.

Don't:
- Persuasion-heavy internal comms — recognized persuasion intent triggers resistance.
- Popularity claims that tell low performers everyone else also cuts corners.
- Assume mandated deployment equals adoption.
